    "seo": {
      "title": "Business Data Analysis in Iran: Questions, Prices and Evidence",
      "description": "Understand business data analysis in Iran through operational questions, data quality and an annual inflation series that clarifies nominal revenue comparisons."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "question",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Begin with an answerable question",
        "content": "Business data analysis investigates a defined question using records, statistical methods, and contextual knowledge. IBM describes business analytics as using operational or publicly available data to uncover patterns and support decisions. In practice, a useful investigation needs a precise subject: which activity, which customers, which period, and which outcome? A broad request to explain poor performance becomes more tractable when narrowed to a particular product line or stage in an operating process. [s1]\n\nConsider an illustrative Iranian wholesaler investigating slower customer payments. Its analysis might separate invoice dates, contractual due dates, receipts, and unpaid balances. These describe different events and should not be treated as interchangeable. This example is a proposed analytical design, not evidence about an actual company. It demonstrates why the question determines which records are needed and what a result could reasonably mean. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "prices",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Revenue growth needs a price context",
        "content": "Iran’s consumer-price record illustrates an important interpretive problem. The World Bank’s annual series reports inflation of 7.2 percent in 2016 and 44.6 percent in 2023, at the precision displayed in DataBank. A rise in money revenue during a period of substantial price increases cannot by itself establish that a business sold more goods. Quantities, selling prices, product mix, and returns are separate questions. [s2]\n\nConsumer-price inflation measures changes in the cost of a consumer basket. It is not a company-specific selling-price index or a universal measure of input costs. The series below supplies economic context; it is not used to calculate real company revenue. FRED’s accompanying World Development Indicators notes identify the consumer-basket concept and the IMF International Financial Statistics source lineage. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "inflation-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: annual consumer-price inflation, 2016–2025",
          "dataset_id": "iran-consumer-price-inflation",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["annual-cpi-change"],
          "x_axis_label": "Source reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Annual change (%)",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The displayed annual rate rose from 7.2% in 2016 to 44.6% in 2023, fell to 32.5% in 2024, and increased to 42.2% in 2025. These are changes in consumer prices, not company revenue or sales volumes. [s2]"
