      {
        "id": "discipline",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Make comparisons reproducible",
        "content": "A defensible analysis records the source, extraction date, coverage, and treatment of missing records. The UK Government Data Quality Framework treats quality assurance as a responsibility across the lifecycle, including communicating limitations to later users. Applied to the wholesaler example, that means documenting whether cancelled invoices, disputed balances, or incomplete receipts were excluded. Another analyst should be able to reconstruct the same comparison. [s4]\n\nThe annual observations here retain the reporting-year labels displayed by the source. They should not be silently relabeled as Iranian Solar Hijri years or matched to a company’s fiscal periods without checking the underlying boundaries. Gregorian start and end dates remain unspecified because the inspected table does not establish them. [s2]\n\nThe strongest conclusion is proportionate to the evidence. An analysis can identify a concentration of overdue invoices and suggest a question for investigation. Demonstrating why those invoices became overdue requires further evidence. For readers assessing Iranian business opportunities, this separation between a measured pattern and an explanation is more informative than a dashboard that implies certainty.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      }
