    "description": "Business intelligence organizes business data into recurring reports and performance measures. Iranian vendor documentation illustrates an enterprise offering, while technical guidance explains why dependable BI requires consistent data models and definitions beneath the dashboard.",
    "tags": ["Business intelligence", "Iran", "Enterprise software", "Dashboards", "Data modeling"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T19:32:06Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T19:32:06Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Business Intelligence in Iran: Reports, Models and Local Evidence",
      "description": "Learn how business intelligence connects records to recurring reports, with an Iranian vendor example and clear limits on claims about adoption and outcomes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "purpose",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A maintained view of business activity",
        "content": "Business intelligence, usually abbreviated to BI, comprises processes and technology for collecting, managing, and analyzing organizational information. A dashboard is one visible output, but the underlying work determines whether its figures mean the same thing across departments and reporting periods. IBM’s explanation places BI within operational and strategic decision-making, rather than treating it as chart production alone. [s1]\n\nA useful editorial distinction is that focused business analysis answers a particular question, while BI maintains information people need repeatedly. An illustrative sales director might consult the same report each month, using consistent definitions to compare products or branches. That continuity is valuable only if users understand the measure, its coverage, and when the underlying records were refreshed. This example describes a use case rather than a verified Iranian deployment. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "local-offering",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An Iranian enterprise-software example",
        "content": "System Group, called Hamkaran System in Persian, describes a BI solution associated with its Rahkaran business software. An inspected Persian-language brochure presents reporting dashboards, information integration, and multidimensional analysis. It also describes financial, sales, logistics, and human-resources analytical content. This shows how a domestic supplier frames BI around connected business functions. [s2]\n\nThe brochure is useful primary evidence of advertised capabilities. Its publication date was not established, and the inspected copy is hosted by Dorsasoft rather than the vendor’s own domain. It therefore cannot establish current licensing, supported versions, market share, or independent customer outcomes. Readers should distinguish the documented product proposition from claims about how widely or effectively BI operates across Iran. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "model",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why the model beneath the dashboard matters",
        "content": "Microsoft’s Power BI guidance explains a common analytical design called a star schema. Fact tables contain events or observations; dimension tables supply categories such as product, place, or date. The level represented by each fact row is its grain. Keeping that grain consistent is central to interpretable summaries. [s3]\n\nFor example, an invoice line and a monthly branch target represent different levels of detail. Combining them without a deliberate model can produce misleading comparisons. Measures also need appropriate aggregation: a rate should not automatically be treated like an additive sales amount. These are general modeling issues, not findings about any Iranian supplier’s implementation. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reporting-chain",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "From operational records to recurring reports",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Operational records\"] -->|\"Extract and prepare\"| B[\"Analytical storage\"]\n  B -->|\"Organize facts and dimensions\"| C[\"Shared analytical model\"]\n  C -->|\"Apply defined measures\"| D[\"Reports and dashboards\"]\n  D -->|\"Interpret for recurring decisions\"| E[\"Business users\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This conceptual reporting chain separates preparation, storage, modeling, and presentation. It combines general technical guidance with capabilities described in the Iranian vendor brochure; it is not an audited architecture of a specific installation.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Assessing a report’s usefulness",
        "content": "A practical review can start with a familiar figure and trace it back to its records. What does it count? Which categories does it include? Can a user distinguish the business event date from the date the report was updated? These questions translate the modeling principles into an inspectable reporting practice. They are proposed evaluation questions, not a claim that one architecture suits every organization. [s3]\n\nFor an international reader, the most useful conclusion is modest: Iranian vendor material documents an enterprise BI offering, and established technical guidance explains how such systems can be structured. Neither source establishes national adoption or guaranteed improvements. A convincing account of a particular project would additionally need implementation evidence and measured outcomes.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
